 hand one that goes like this  any ideas what that gesture might mean  in emotional terms if i were to guess to  me it looks like it's bo  it's counterintuitive because it's a  halt it's a don't come closer sign and  then a bit of an invitation so i i'm  curious i don't know yeah it's a funny  one uh and this actually to the romans  indicated wonder or amazement so you  kind of get a sense of that so some of  these seem very intrinsic like kind of  the fist against the chest everybody  would see his grief this one's a little  more artificial but if you've been  trained to sort of know that gesture  know what it meant it would probably  register as wonder amazement to you  think about how music is used in movies  today so if you're watching a horror  film they'll use sharp kind of strident  chords which make you feel tense or  nervous if it's a love story they'll use  strings to kind of soothe you and make  you feel romantic well cicero was very  well aware of this and he said that the  same way that certain musical tones can  make you feel  happy sad tense whatever certain  postures of the body or gestures will  make you feel certain emotions a  question here that  i'm wondering is how much did the  audience actually know  about these gestures that these master  orators were using  were they  well-versed in what these gestures meant  or were they just supposed to  feel  based off of the actions themselves  yeah that's a great question in fact  that's one of the big questions i had to  look into in my book and it looks like  there was a kind of vocabulary of  gesture that was known by a lot of  audiences and this came from two  directions one was that public speeches  were almost a form of entertainment in  ancient rome i mean there were people  who would just go hang out in the forum  to watch kind of the entertaining  orators talk to the crowds and also a  lot of roman law cases were not held in  um you know courtroom hidden away they  too were held out in the forum and  crowds would come and watch these things  in the late republic especially there  were a lot of what today would call  celebrity court trials where very famous  men were put on trial and everybody  would gather to watch these things and  the orators were very aware that they  had to play to the crowd uh that  interaction with the crowd was a huge  part of trying to win the court cases  and so the crowds would know this kind  of vocabulary of gesture and they would  know them not just from politics and the  law system but from entertainment as  well  how have gestures from  ancient rome evolved what do we still  see today are there any echoes today  that are still being used that were  developed you know during this this uh  period well the basic kind of  rules for effective public speaking are  are seemingly timeless i mean the same  sorts of things that cicero advocates  which were very successful in antiquity  you can see are what we tend to point to  in orders today where we consider very  effective orders and this is down to  very specific things i mean there's all  this stuff about you know you should  gesture between the level of your eyes  your waist your right hand should be the  dominant hand of gesture the left should  mimic the right uh there's all these  sort of very technical things but if we  even just think about uh delivery which  is uh the whole how you give a speech um  everybody probably knows from personal  experience or school with teachers that  you could have a very mediocre speech in  terms of content but if it's given in an  inspired exciting way you're pretty  entertained and enjoy that and you pay  attention whereas on the other hand you  might have a brilliantly written speech  but if the delivery is dull and boring  it's just not effective  and it's also true that the  practitioners of outstanding delivery  and maybe this is an unfortunate aspect  of public speaking is that it's possible  to use those sorts of skills for very  different moral purposes and we see this  with a lot of  sort of people regarded as famous  orators from the last let's say 100  years  so if we looked at two figures like um  martin luther king and hitler  both of them were highly effective  orators  both of them used a lot of gestures when  they spoke and both were absolute  masters of arousing their audience's  emotions so it's back to that core  concept of cicero but of course one man  used all those talents for good and one  use them for evil  so oratory is a tricky thing i mean it's  clear what makes a good oratory but it's  not always used for the best purposes  greg thank you so much for joining us  today where can people learn more about  you and the work that you do  well it's a fun conversation i like  talking about this stuff and if people  are curious about my work uh check out  my website uh i have a website that's  gregorysaldrete.com no spaces so just  gregory saldrete.com  and you'll see what i've done and what  i'm working on  all right greg i will say this as i  perform the hand gesture of wonderment  it is great to talk to you and we'll  talk again soon thank you enjoyed the  conversation  and that'll do it for another episode of  one dream now to learn who our upcoming  guests are and to ask them questions  make sure to keep an eye on our social  media feeds you can find links to all of  them in the description below  we'll be back soon with another great  show be sure to subscribe so you don't  miss it
 

